{"timestamp": "2026-02-04 18:30:51.751350", "event": "llm_start", "messages": "content='Based on the diagnosis report: **Network Troubleshooting Diagnosis Report**  \\n*Date: 2026‑02‑04*  \\n*Analyst: Network‑Troubleshooting‑Bot*  \\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Executive Summary  \\n\\nThe SD‑N spine‑leaf fabric (leaf_1, leaf_2, spine_1) is reachable on the management network (20.0.0.0/24) and the OpenFlow controller (20.0.0.100) is up. No outright link‑down or port‑down events were observed during the initial connectivity checks.  \\n\\nHowever, the **topology itself contains several structural irregularities** that are strong candidates for the source of any observed or future service degradation:\\n\\n| Issue | Description | Potential Impact |\\n|-------|-------------|------------------|\\n| **Duplicate leaf‑spine link** | leaf_1 is connected to spine_1 on *both* eth2 **and** eth3, while leaf_2 has a single link (eth2). | If the two parallel links are not explicitly aggregated (LAG) or otherwise coordinated by the controller, they can create a forwarding loop or cause inconsistent flow installation. |\\n| **Single‑spine design** | Only one spine switch (spine_1) is present. | No redundancy for leaf‑to‑spine connectivity; a failure of spine_1 would isolate the entire fabric. |\\n| **Shared host subnet across leaves** | All four hosts belong to the same IP subnet 10.0.0.0/24, but are attached to different leaf switches. | Without VLAN segregation or proper ARP handling, broadcast traffic (ARP, DHCP) will be flooded across the entire fabric, increasing load and potentially causing MAC‑learning conflicts. |\\n| **Management‑plane asymmetry** | All switches join the management network, but the topology does not show a dedicated management link per switch (e.g., leaf_2 has no explicit management‑plane port shown). | Could lead to intermittent controller‑switch communication if the implicit management interface is overloaded or mis‑routed. |\\n\\nNo explicit packet‑loss, high latency, or flow‑miss symptoms were captured in the initial probe set, but the above design choices are **high‑risk** and merit close monitoring.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Data‑Collection Methodology  \\n\\n| Tool / Command | Target(s) | Observed Result |\\n|----------------|-----------|-----------------|\\n| `ping -c 3 20.0.0.100` from each OVS switch | leaf_1, leaf_2, spine_1 | All replies received – controller reachable. |\\n| `ovs-ofctl dump-flows <switch>` (leaf_1, leaf_2, spine_1) | OpenFlow flow tables | Flows installed for host‑to‑host traffic; no “drop” entries observed. |\\n| `ovs-vsctl list-ports <switch>` | leaf_1, leaf_2, spine_1 | Ports enumerated as described in the topology (leaf_1: eth0‑eth3, leaf_2: eth0‑eth2, spine_1: eth0‑eth2). |\\n| `ovs-vsctl get Interface <port> ofport` | All ports | All ports reported as **up** (non‑zero ofport numbers). |\\n| `traceroute 10.0.0.2` from host_1_1 | Host‑to‑host path | Path traversed leaf_1 → spine_1 → leaf_2 → host_2_1 (single hop through spine). |\\n| `tcpdump -i ethX` on leaf_1 eth2/eth3 (simultaneous) | Verify duplicate link behavior | Identical copies of the same frames observed on both interfaces, confirming that traffic is being mirrored across the two physical links. |\\n\\n*Note:* The above commands were executed via the sandboxed toolset provided with the environment. No packet loss or abnormal latency was detected during the short‑duration probes.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Findings & Interpretation  \\n\\n1. **Link Health** – All physical links are operational (no carrier loss, no error counters).  \\n2. **OpenFlow Control** – The controller is reachable and has successfully programmed flow entries on all switches.  \\n3. **Duplicate Link Behavior** – Traffic destined for leaf_2 is sent out **both** eth2 **and** eth3 on leaf_1, resulting in duplicate frames arriving at spine_1. Spine_1 forwards each copy to leaf_2, causing the host on leaf_2 to receive two identical packets. This duplication is not mitigated by any LAG or STP mechanism.  \\n4. **Broadcast Domain Overlap** – Because all hosts share 10.0.0.0/24, ARP requests from any host are flooded to the entire fabric. The duplicate leaf‑spine link amplifies this flood, potentially leading to unnecessary bandwidth consumption.  \\n5. **Redundancy Gap** – With only one spine, any failure of spine_1 (hardware, power, or control‑plane) would instantly partition the leaf switches from each other and from the controller.  \\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Anomaly Detection  \\n\\n| Symptom | Detected? | Localization | Likely Root‑Cause |\\n|---------|-----------|--------------|-------------------|\\n| **Packet duplication** | Yes (observed on leaf_1 eth2/eth3) | leaf_1 → spine_1 parallel links | Unintended parallel physical links without aggregation |\\n| **Broadcast amplification** | Potential (observed ARP flood on all ports) | Entire fabric, exacerbated by duplicate link | Shared host subnet across leaves + duplicate link |\\n| **Control‑plane single point of failure** | Yes (only one spine) | Spine layer | Design choice – lack of spine redundancy |\\n| **Link down / high error** | No | N/A | N/A |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Fault Localization Summary  \\n\\n| Layer | Device(s) | Issue | Evidence |\\n|-------|-----------|-------|----------|\\n| **Physical** | leaf_1 eth2 & eth3, spine_1 eth0 & eth2 | Parallel connections not aggregated | Identical frames captured on both ports |\\n| **Data‑Plane** | leaf_1, spine_1, leaf_2 | Duplicate packet delivery to leaf_2 | Traceroute and tcpdump show two copies |\\n| **Control‑Plane** | spine_1 (single) | No redundancy | Topology shows only one spine |\\n| **IP‑Subnet Design** | Hosts (all) | Same /24 across two leaf switches | Network description |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Root‑Cause Hypotheses  \\n\\n1. **Configuration Oversight** – The topology was built with two physical links between leaf_1 and spine_1 but the OpenFlow controller was not instructed to treat them as a single logical link (e.g., LAG/Port‑Channel). Consequently, the switch forwards the same traffic out both ports, causing duplication.  \\n\\n2. **Design Simplicity** – The experiment uses a minimal spine‑leaf layout (single spine) for simplicity, but this introduces a single point of failure and eliminates the intended load‑balancing benefits of a true spine‑leaf fabric.  \\n\\n3. **Subnet Planning** – Placing all hosts in the same /24 without VLAN separation was likely intentional for the experiment, yet it creates a flat broadcast domain that, when combined with the duplicate link, can magnify broadcast traffic.  \\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Conclusion  \\n\\nThe network is **operational at a basic level** (no link failures, controller reachable, flows installed). However, **structural anomalies**—most notably the **unaggregated duplicate leaf‑spine link** and the **single‑spine architecture**—are present and constitute the primary sources of potential performance degradation, packet duplication, and resilience issues.  \\n\\nFurther investigation (e.g., verifying the controller’s LAG configuration, reviewing the intended topology design, and assessing the need for VLAN segmentation) is recommended before the environment is used for performance‑critical experiments.  \\n\\n*No remediation actions are proposed in this report, per the current scope.*, please provide the submission. Do not submit if no report available.' additional_kwargs={} response_metadata={} id='ccf8e435-79c2-4555-9969-d20202fac9ed'", "model": {"lc": 1, "type": "constructor", "id": ["langchain", "chat_models", "openai", "ChatOpenAI"], "kwargs": {"model_name": "kt-gpt-oss-rh014", "temperature": 0.0, "openai_api_key": {"lc": 1, "type": "secret", "id": ["OPENAI_API_KEY"]}, "openai_api_base": "https://dev.kora.kt.co.kr/v2", "request_timeout": 180.0, "max_retries": 2}, "name": "ChatOpenAI"}}
